---
/**
 * /tools/ppn-002-calculator/methodology
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS NOW AND NOT BEFORE. The port was deliberately withheld while
 * the legacy page described a calculator that does not exist: NPV, an HM
 * Treasury Green Book 3.5% discount rate, National TOMs proxy values mapped
 * "directly into the calculation", £27,000 per sustained job, and a three-tier
 * scoring window. The engine (lib/ppn002.ts) contains none of those — zero
 * occurrences of any of them. Porting it would have carried the fabrication
 * onto the new site. That content is now removed at source, so the page can
 * ship.
 *
 * THIS WAS ALSO A DEAD LINK ON EVERY PAGE. The route is reached from the
 * footer, so all 38 pages in the build linked to it. It survived the link
 * checker because `_redirects` carries a 200 REWRITE with this path on the
 * left-hand side, and the first version of that checker treated any rule
 * source as proof a link worked. A rewrite is not a redirect: it serves a file
 * directly, and the file it names does not exist in the Astro build. The
 * checker now follows a rule's destination.
 *
 * THE CONTENT RULE THIS PAGE IS UNDER. It may only describe what
 * lib/ppn002.ts actually computes: three inputs, one constant (FLOOR_PCT = 10)
 * and two lines of arithmetic. It must not imply the tool implements the TOMs
 * measure set, monetises anything, or discounts a cashflow — it has no year
 * input and no currency formatter. And it must never assert compliance:
 * whether a procurement is compliant is the contracting authority's
 * judgement, which is why the tool reports a floor check and stops.
 */
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../../../components/layout/Section.astro';
import Button from '../../../components/ui/Button.astro';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../../../lib/schema';
import { SITE } from '../../../data/site';
import { FLOOR_PCT } from '../../../lib/ppn002';

const title = 'PPN 002 Methodology | How the 10% floor is calculated | CrowAgent';
const description =
  'How the free PPN 002 calculator works: what PPN 002 changed, why the minimum social value weighting is 10%, and the exact arithmetic the tool performs.';

const schema = [
  // TechArticle. The legacy methodology page published one; the port did not
  // carry it over because the page was rebuilt from scratch (OA-24).
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: title,
    description,
    url: `${SITE.origin}/tools/ppn-002-calculator/methodology`,
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'CrowAgent', url: `${SITE.origin}/` },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'CrowAgent', url: `${SITE.origin}/` },
  },
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Tools', path: '/tools' },
    { name: 'PPN 002 Calculator', path: '/tools/ppn-002-calculator' },
    { name: 'Methodology', path: '/tools/ppn-002-calculator/methodology' },
  ]),
];
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/tools/ppn-002-calculator/methodology"
  schema={schema}
>
  <Section
    as="h1"
    eyebrow="Methodology"
    title="Sizing the 10% social value minimum"
    standfirst="How PPN 002 sets a 10% minimum social value weighting, where the TOMs framework fits, and exactly how this calculator checks a bid against that floor."
    id="methodology"
  >
    <div class="meth">
      <h2 id="what-changed">PPN 002: what changed, and when</h2>
      <p>
        PPN 002 was published on 13 February 2025 and is mandatory for in-scope procurements from
        1 October 2025. It sets the minimum social value weighting at 10% and supersedes PPN 06/20
        for new procurements, simplifying the routing and fixing the floor.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both rest on the same foundation: a Social Value Model of five themes and eight policy
        outcomes, scored using the National TOMs Framework. What PPN 002 changes is that the 10%
        becomes a floor rather than a recommendation.
      </p>

      <h2 id="the-minimum">The 10% minimum</h2>
      <p>
        10% is the minimum weighting on social value within the overall award criteria. It is a
        floor, not a ceiling: buying organisations frequently set higher weightings of 15%, 20%,
        occasionally 25%, where the contract has a clear social value angle.
      </p>
      <p>
        This calculator checks your proposal against the 10% floor. It does not detect or flag a
        higher weighting the authority has set, because it never sees the authority's criteria,
        only the numbers you enter.
      </p>

      <h2 id="toms">Where the TOMs framework fits</h2>
      <p>
        TOMs (Themes, Outcomes, Measures) is the de-facto scoring rubric. The National TOMs
        Framework lists Measures grouped by Outcome and Theme, and each Measure carries a
        quantifiable indicator, "number of FTE local employees hired" for instance, and a proxy value.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is background on how social value is scored in UK procurement generally.
        <strong>This calculator does not implement the TOMs measure set or its proxy values.</strong>
        It checks the weighting floor and nothing else. Monetising commitments against proxy values
        is done in CrowMark, aligned to National TOMs conventions rather than as a full National
        TOMs implementation.
      </p>

      <h2 id="the-arithmetic">What the calculator computes</h2>
      <p>
        The whole method is two lines of arithmetic, printed here because a page called
        "methodology" should show its working.
      </p>

      <dl class="meth-eq">
        <div>
          <dt>The floor, in points</dt>
          <dd>
            <code>floor = total weighting × {FLOOR_PCT}%</code>
            <p>On a 100-point award criteria set, the floor is 10 points.</p>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Your share, as a percentage</dt>
          <dd>
            <code>share = (proposed social value ÷ total weighting) × 100</code>
            <p>That number is what gets compared against {FLOOR_PCT}.</p>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <p>
        If your share is below {FLOOR_PCT}%, the shortfall is the difference in points. That is the
        entire calculation. Three inputs, the government mission, your total weighting and your
        proposed social value weighting. One constant, the {FLOOR_PCT}% floor. Outputs in
        percentages and points.
      </p>

      <h2 id="not">What it does not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not certify compliance.</strong> Whether a bid or a procurement meets
          PPN 002 is a judgement for the contracting authority. The tool reports whether a number
          clears the floor, and stops.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not work in pounds.</strong> There is no currency in the calculation. It
          returns percentages and points.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not discount over time.</strong> There is no year or duration input, so
          there is nothing to discount and no time horizon to model.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not write the bid or track delivery.</strong> Those live in CrowMark.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="references">References</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Procurement Policy Note 002 (PPN 002), published 13 February 2025, mandatory from 1 October 2025.</li>
        <li>National TOMs Framework.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="meth-cta">
        <Button href="/tools/ppn-002-calculator">Run the calculator</Button>
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Long-form measure. The shared .prose stylesheet is for Markdown output;
     this page is authored, so it carries its own narrow block rather than
     inheriting rules written for a different source. */
  /* `margin-inline: auto` is the whole fix. Measured at 1440 before it: the
     head block centred at 0px and this column, the entire article, sat 189px
     left with 426px of empty space on the right. The page read as
     left-aligned even though every heading declared `text-align: center`. */
  .meth { max-width: 68ch; margin-inline: auto; }

  .meth h2 {
    margin-top: var(--sec-gap);
    margin-bottom: var(--stack-1);
    padding-top: var(--stack-2);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    /* --t-h2, matched to styles/prose.css. This page hand-copies the .prose
       long-form recipe, and it copied the one-tier slip with it: a document
       section heading rendering at the sub-heading size. */
    font-size: var(--t-h2);
    font-weight: var(--t-w-h2);
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    color: var(--c-text);
    scroll-margin-top: 96px;
  }

  .meth p {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    font-size: var(--t-lede);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .meth strong { color: var(--c-text); font-weight: 600; }

  .meth ul {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    padding-left: 1.35em;
    list-style: disc;
  }

  .meth li {
    margin-bottom: var(--stack-1);
    font-size: var(--t-lede);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .meth li::marker { color: var(--c-text-muted); }

  /* The two equations. A <dl> because each is a term and its definition, and
     the pairing is the point. */
  .meth-eq {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--stack-2);
  }

  .meth-eq > div {
    padding: var(--blk-pad);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--c-teal);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius-card) var(--radius-card) 0;
    background: var(--c-card);
  }

  .meth-eq dt {
    margin-bottom: var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    line-height: var(--lh-tight);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-teal);
  }

  .meth-eq dd { margin: 0; }

  .meth-eq code {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--c-text);
    /* A formula has no spaces to break at, so it must be allowed to scroll
       rather than push the page sideways at 390px. */
    overflow-x: auto;
    max-width: 100%;
  }

  .meth-eq dd p {
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--t-body);
  }

  .meth-cta { margin-top: var(--sec-gap); }
</style>
